  Critical gaps

  1. Prompt defers the code. Its last line says "Full implementation code will be developed in subsequent phases." The project requires actual Terraform and Helm.
     Following the prompt as written gives you a design doc only.
  2. No public repo requirement. The prompt doesn't cover repo layout, README, sanitization, or a sample customer. It also assumes GitHub Enterprise, while the
     project says a public repo.
  3. Public repo plus self-hosted runners is a security risk. The prompt has "anyone can submit PRs" and self-hosted GitHub Actions runners. On a public repo, a
     fork PR can run code on your runners. The prompt needs a constraint on this, such as GitHub-hosted runners for the demo or approval-gated workflows.
  4. Cloud Run and Instance Group are under-specified. Almost every locked decision is GKE-only: Istio, Argo Rollouts, namespaces, NetworkPolicy, Velero, Usage
     Metering.
     - The resource hierarchy has no project for Cloud Run or MIG workloads.
     - Tenant isolation for those two runtimes isn't defined.
     - Zero-downtime isn't covered for them (Cloud Run revisions and traffic split, MIG rolling update).
     - Their ingress path isn't defined either, because "Apigee → Istio" doesn't apply.
     - Packer and Ansible appear only in assumptions.
     - Two of your three required diagrams therefore have little to draw from.
  5. Tenancy model differs from the scenario. The scenario says one customer's products share "the same infrastructure/project". The prompt locks shared
     multi-customer projects, with a dedicated project only for enterprise customers. Either state this as a deliberate interpretation or add a
     per-customer-project option.
  6. Version divergence is shallow. The prompt handles version pins and config overrides. It doesn't say how customer-specific customizations are built or packaged
     (feature flags, image variants, forks). It also doesn't say how schema migrations work on a shared instance when customers sit on different versions. And a
     dev environment on 2.6.0-beta reading prod data on 2.5.0 is unaddressed.

  Internal contradictions to fix

  - Cloning vs isolation. Decision 3 puts schema-per-tenant on a shared Cloud SQL instance, and Decision 11 clones that instance. A clone copies every customer's
    data into non-prod, which breaks the isolation and "no prod data in non-prod" goals. The prompt needs a per-customer clone path.
  - Cloud SQL placement. Section 12 says infrastructure is "per-product per-customer by default". The example provisions "Cloud SQL (payment-api)" per customer.
    Decision 3 says shared instances. Pick one.
  - Certificates. Dev and stage use self-signed certificates in Decision 7, but "Google-managed or Let's Encrypt" for non-prod in the certificate section. Let's
    Encrypt also can't validate *.platform.internal.
  - Non-prod cluster type. "Zonal GKE clusters (3 zones)" is ambiguous. Zonal and regional are different cluster types.
  - Tier naming. Sizing tiers are Small/Medium/Large and HA tiers are Standard/Enterprise, with no mapping between them. Small is 99.5% SLA, but the success
