  1. Executing the prompt (what Claude produces)

  The prompt produces files in a repo, not real GCP resources. Nothing is applied to GCP for this project. Everything is validated offline (terraform validate,
  helm lint, helm template).

  The 4 phases are:
  1. Design and the three diagrams
  2. Terraform
  3. Helm
  4. Supporting artifacts and repo packaging

  Claude writes the repo in that order. The ordering questions you're asking about are about how the platform it designs would run.

  2. Executing the platform (the order your dependencies imply)

  The Terraform needs to be split into layers. Each layer is its own state and runs after the one above it.

  L0 Bootstrap (manual, once)
     org, billing, state bucket, CI service account, GitHub→GCP identity federation
          ↓
  L1 Foundation (once)
     folders, nonprod + prod projects, Shared VPC, NAT, Cloud Armor,
     VPC-SC perimeters, org policies, Artifact Registry, platform-services (ArgoCD, runners)
          ↓
  L2 Regional platform (once per region)
     GKE nonprod + prod cluster pair, shared Cloud SQL / BigQuery / GCS,
     Cloud Run + MIG baselines, Istio, Velero, DR (replicas, backups, secondary region)
          ↓   publishes → clusters/<name>.yaml  (the inventory)
  L3 Customer (once per customer, in a loop)
     namespaces, schemas, service accounts + Workload Identity, IAM, quotas,
     Redis/Pub/Sub/KMS... only for enabled products
          ↓
  L4 Product delivery (every release)
     ArgoCD ApplicationSet, Cloud Run revision, MIG rollout

  Layers L0–L2 are platform work, done by the platform team and rarely changed. L3–L4 are what a customer PR triggers.

  3. How do we know what exists and where to onboard?

  Git is the registry. Nobody has to query GCP.

  - What was created: each L2 apply writes an inventory file, e.g. clusters/nonprod-us-east1-01.yaml. It holds region, environment class, project, cluster name,
    shared SQL instance names, capacity cap, and status: active.
  - Where to onboard: a customer PR sets only region.primary. The placement job then:
    a. reads clusters/*.yaml where region matches and status: active
    b. counts how many customers/*/customer.yaml already point at each cluster
    c. picks the pair with the most headroom and writes it back into customer.yaml
  - If the region has no cluster: validation fails with "region not provisioned". This enforces the order, so a customer can't be onboarded before L2 exists.
  - Which products to deploy: products.yaml is read against the catalog product.yaml. Terraform creates the infrastructure, then ArgoCD, Cloud Run or the MIG
    pipeline deploys the workload.

  Customer L3 Terraform reads the cluster and database names from the inventory files, so L3 never hard-codes anything from L2.

  4. Gap in the prompt

  The prompt doesn't currently say any of this explicitly:
  - the layer split (L0–L4) and their apply order
  - the clusters/*.yaml inventory and how L2 publishes it
  - the "region not provisioned" check

