  There are three ways to house 200 customers:

  ┌────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              Way               │                        Analogy                         │                     Problem                     │
  ├────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ One building per customer      │ 200+ buildings (800+ if each environment gets its own) │ Very expensive, and hard to maintain            │
  ├────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Everyone in one giant building │ A single huge building, with locked apartments         │ Cheap, but a fire or flood hits everyone        │
  ├────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Several medium buildings       │ About 20 buildings, with 25-40 customers each          │ Good balance. A problem hits only one building. │
  └────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Our design is the third way. The "medium building" is called a cell. It is a set of clusters, databases and networking that serves 25-40 customers.

  A very demanding customer (for example, one with strict legal requirements) can have their own building. That is the isolation: dedicated option. It is rare, so
  it won't create hundreds of projects.

  How many buildings will we manage?

  About 100-150 projects in total, not 800. That's a normal number for a platform team. It stays manageable because one template (the project factory) creates
  every project the same way, with the same rules, budget and logging already set up.

  How customers stay separate inside a shared building

  Inside a shared building, each customer gets their own locked apartment:

  - Own namespace and database schema. This is the apartment door.
  - Own encryption key. This is a personal safe. If the customer leaves, you destroy the key and their data becomes unreadable forever.
  - Own storage bucket, instead of sharing one bucket with folders. This is a private storage unit instead of a shared closet with labelled shelves. It is easier
    to prove to an auditor that nobody else can get in.
  - Automatic tests that try to break in. For example, "can customer A read customer B's data?" The test must fail every time.

