● Of the two you suggest, one ArgoCD per cluster is the better fit. This reverses my last recommendation of two hubs, and I'll explain why below.

  Is there an industry standard?

  No single standard exists. I'm summarising common community guidance rather than citing one source. Two points are widely agreed:
  - Keep prod separate from non-prod. Almost everyone does this.
  - Beyond that, there are two models:
    - Hub-and-spoke (one ArgoCD manages many clusters) is simpler for small fleets.
    - One ArgoCD per cluster is more common for larger fleets or when isolation matters, because there is no central credential store and the blast radius is
      smaller.

  The options for your design

  ┌───────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              Option               │    Instances     │                                         Fit for your design                                         │
  ├───────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Per environment (dev, stage, uat, │                  │ Weak. Dev, stage and uat run on the same non-prod clusters, so three ArgoCDs would deploy into the  │
  │  prod)                            │ 4                │ same clusters. It adds instances without adding isolation. Each instance still needs network access │
  │                                   │                  │  and credentials for every cluster.                                                                 │
  ├───────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Per environment class (non-prod,  │ 2                │ Works, but the hubs need a path into every private cluster API, and each hub holds credentials for  │
  │ prod), my last recommendation     │                  │ all clusters.                                                                                       │
  ├───────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Per cluster (recommended)         │ About 15-25, one │ Best fit. See below.                                                                                │
  │                                   │  per cluster     │                                                                                                     │
  └───────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Why per cluster fits better

  - Private clusters. ArgoCD runs inside the cluster it manages and deploys to "in-cluster". No cross-network path, and no stored cluster credentials.
  - Built-in scaling. Each cluster holds 25-40 customers, which is about 1,000 Applications per instance. That is easy for ArgoCD, with no sharding tuning.
  - Smaller blast radius. A broken ArgoCD affects one cluster. Running workloads keep running even if ArgoCD is down.
  - Prod is separate automatically, because prod and non-prod are different clusters.
  - It matches the placement design. The placement job writes each customer's cluster into customer.yaml. Each cluster's ArgoCD ApplicationSet selects only
    customers whose cluster equals its own name. Nothing extra is needed to route customers to the right instance.
  - Onboarding is automatic. L2 Terraform installs ArgoCD when it creates a cluster. ArgoCD then installs everything else from Git (app-of-apps). A new cluster
    joins with no manual steps.

  The costs of per cluster

  1. More instances to upgrade. Mitigation: ArgoCD manages itself from Git, so an upgrade is a version-bump PR. It goes to non-prod clusters first, then prod, the
     same as the cluster upgrade path.
  2. No single UI. Mitigation: export ArgoCD metrics to Cloud Monitoring and alert on out-of-sync Applications. You can add an aggregated read-only dashboard
     later.
  3. Rollout waves across customers (a new version to 10 customers first, then more) can't use a central ApplicationSet. They are driven by Git: CI changes the
     version pins in batches, with PRs per wave.
  4. A little extra resource use per cluster for the ArgoCD pods.

  I recommended two hubs earlier mainly because only 5 engineers will run this. That worry only holds if each instance is set up by hand. With L2 installing ArgoCD
  automatically and ArgoCD upgrading itself from Git, the per-instance effort is close to zero. The private-cluster networking that hubs need is also a cost I
  hadn't fully counted.

  What I'd add to the md

  - An "ArgoCD topology" section with one instance per cluster, installed by L2 and self-managed from Git.
  - Each cluster's ApplicationSet filters customers by their written-back cluster name.
  - One AppProject per customer.
  - Create/update-only in prod, with no deletes from a bad generator.
  - manifest-generate-paths and webhooks.
  - Metrics and out-of-sync alerts to Cloud Monitoring.
  - Rollout waves driven by version-pin PRs.

