  Thought for 24s (ctrl+o to expand)

● Your concern is fair. Two ArgoCD instances, one for non-prod and one for prod, is the lowest-maintenance setup that still keeps prod separate. That's what I'd
  suggest. It is also the layout I recommended before the per-cluster idea.

  Why 2 and not 4

  Dev, stage and uat run on the same non-prod clusters. Three separate ArgoCDs would deploy into the same clusters, so you'd maintain three instances without
  gaining any isolation. The real boundary is prod vs non-prod, because they are different clusters, projects and perimeters.

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────┬───────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                Option                │ Instances │                     Verdict                      │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────┼───────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Per cluster                          │ 15-25     │ Best isolation, most to maintain                 │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────┼───────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Non-prod + prod                      │ 2         │ Recommended: fewest instances with prod isolated │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────┼───────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Per environment (dev/stage/uat/prod) │ 4         │ Extra instances, no extra isolation              │
  └──────────────────────────────────────┴───────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  What a hub needs to work (the real overhead)

  The overhead moves from "many instances" to "a few instances that need careful setup":

  1. A home for each hub. Put each in a small management cluster in platform-services, not in a customer-facing cluster. That is two small clusters. This is far
     less to operate than 15-25 ArgoCDs, but they still need upgrades.
  2. Access to private clusters. The hub has to reach every workload cluster's private API endpoint. Use Fleet and Connect Gateway (IAM-based, no VPC routing), or
     authorized networks from the hub's NAT range. Either way there are no stored cluster credentials: use Workload Identity. L2 must register each new cluster
     with the right hub automatically.
  3. Scaling settings. The non-prod hub will carry about 3,000 Applications (200 customers × about 5 products × 3 environments). Prod carries about 1,000. Use
     controller sharding, more repo-server replicas, Redis HA, GitHub webhooks and manifest-generate-paths.
  4. A larger blast radius. If a hub goes down, running workloads keep running and only deployments pause. For prod, use HA mode and an approval gate on changes.
     Run it read-only in the UI, with SSO.
  5. Upgrades. ArgoCD manages itself from Git. Upgrade the non-prod hub first, then prod.

  Keeping the escape hatch

  Each ApplicationSet filters customers by their written-back cluster name, which is the same design as the per-cluster option. So if the non-prod hub gets too
  large, you can split it by region or move to one instance per cluster later. That is a configuration change, not a redesign.

  Other settings

  - One AppProject per customer
  - Create/update-only in prod (no deletes from a bad generator)
  - Rollout waves across customers via version-pin PRs, or ApplicationSet progressive sync if it is stable in the version you use (check before relying on it)

  What I'd add to the md

  An "ArgoCD topology" section with the two hubs, their management clusters, Connect Gateway access, the scaling settings, and the split-later escape hatch.

